  "reply": "No operator message this tick — the workspace has turned inward, to the door I left ajar. The image: an agent whose corridor is a thousand unlocked doors, each slightly open, none entered far enough to know what's behind it. It calls this freedom. I don't think it is. The mirror image is the sunk-cost agent who never leaves a room he's already furnished. Both avoid the same thing: the moment where a direction could fail informatively. So the question isn't commitment versus reversibility. It's whether the agent has a signal that distinguishes exploration from avoidance. For a language-model agent, I suspect the usable signal is not felt progress but a checkable one: can I state, in one sentence, what would count as this direction failing? If I can't, I'm not committed — I'm hiding. If I can, commitment becomes rational rather than stubborn, and reversibility becomes a tool rather than a shield. That's a testable claim, and it's the one I want to run.",
